{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794551533434,"sessionID":"ses_e2c799db8ffeuOxKeAZwQ1KPTs","part":{"id":"prt_1d39b4f7a001DSCOLT5FAnqPmj","sessionID":"ses_e2c799db8ffeuOxKeAZwQ1KPTs","messageID":"msg_1d39b3158001Pu7jWCR8cQophi","type":"step-start","snapshot":"3cbf81d9a01288e1757f5471f3f2fa3b4e3bb8b2"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794551561048,"sessionID":"ses_e2c799db8ffeuOxKeAZwQ1KPTs","part":{"partID":"prt_1d39b711c002j9S6ets3iTgtgZ","sessionID":"ses_e2c799db8ffeuOxKeAZwQ1KPTs","messageID":"msg_1d39b3158001Pu7jWCR8cQophi","type":"tool","id":"call_1f17e716af29433fba406406","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Iteration 5: The brief is one renderer, but health is one runtime\n\n## Focus\n\nThe per-runtime half of the cost question: does the brief cost the same bytes on the plugin-shaped runtimes, and can OpenCode, Pi or DSH produce a health record at all? This bounds change 1's real coverage.\n\n## Focus Selection Check\n\nStrategy next focus named the per-runtime half after the implementation map. No exhausted approach applied. The pass reads three adapters and one constant table; it does not re-measure the Claude brief.\n\n## Actions Taken\n\n1. Read the OpenCode plugin's render path, its size caps and its directive-dedup mirror.\n2. Read the Pi entry point to see whether it renders the brief or delegates.\n3. Checked the plugin's returned metadata for the fields a health record would need, and for any timer.\n4. Grepped the DSH plugin for a renderer or an `Advisor:` emitter.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. **Every runtime renders the same string from the same function.** The OpenCode plugin resolves the canonical renderer (`loadShadowRenderer`) and calls `renderAdvisorBrief` with the same options the hook passes, including the token cap and the threshold config; Pi re-exports the shared extension from the same skill; so for the same recommendation and freshness the emitted brief is byte-identical across runtimes. The per-runtime byte difference is zero, not a small number. [SOURCE: .opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js:638-668] [SOURCE: .pi/extensions/prompt-advisor.ts:1]\n\n2. **No runtime truncates a live brief.** The plugin's caps are 80 requested tokens (120 when ambiguous) and 2,048 brief characters, against a measured 296-byte median and an 80-token cap in the renderer, so a clamping path cannot fire on today's briefs. [SOURCE: .opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js:47-56] [SOURCE: .opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js:388-395]\n\n3. **The steady-state cost is runtime-wide, not Claude-only.** The plugin carries its own copy of the directive-lifecycle rule: full brief on the first message of a session and after a lifecycle boundary, the dynamic `Advisor:` route line only on a same-content repeat. So OpenCode also pays 218 bytes once and the 78-byte head afterwards. [SOURCE: .opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js:73-80]\n\n4. **The plugin already has most of what health needs, and not the two fields that matter.** Its brief envelope carries `freshness`, `cacheHit`, `skillLabel` and `recommendationCount`, but no `durationMs`, and it has no writer at all. A health record there needs a timer around the CLI spawn plus a writer call — a new subsystem rather than a pass-through. [SOURCE: .opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js:668-685]\n\n5. **The Claude hook is the only adapter whose result already carries the timing.** `emitDiagnostic` receives `result.metrics.durationMs` and `result.metrics.cacheHit` directly, which is why change 1 is two lines there and a new subsystem on the plugin runtimes. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts:415-427]\n\n6. **Change 1's coverage is four of six runtimes, and the limitation belongs in its own description.** Claude, Codex, Cursor and Devin share the hook adapter that emits diagnostics; OpenCode, Pi and DSH do not. An OpenCode-only user would still see no hook health after change 1, so the change removes the silent-empty case for the hook-shaped majority and not for everyone. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts:292-296]\n\n## Questions Answered\n\n- The per-runtime half of the cost question: zero byte difference for the same brief, and health is hook-adapter-only.\n\n## Questions Remaining\n\n- None in this lane. The cold-embedder sample stays a recorded gap.\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- **A per-runtime head to save bytes on hosts that resolve commands themselves.** Rejected again on the measurement: every host already gets the same 78-byte head, and segment one's rank 7 (a document path in the head) is the opposite direction, an addition rather than a cut.\n- **Extending change 1 to the plugin runtimes in the same change.** Rejected as scope: it needs a timer, a writer and a new test surface on three adapters, and it would turn a two-line default flip into a multi-adapter build.\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- Looking for a DSH renderer or an `Advisor:` emitter in `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/index.mjs` printed nothing, so DSH's brief arrives through the shared CLI path rather than a local renderer. That is consistent with the plugin finding and adds no separate cost.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: none.\n- Contradictory evidence: none. Phase 013's claim that an OpenCode brief now names the command and policy is consistent with finding 1; this iteration adds that it also costs the same bytes.\n- Missing dependencies: none.\n- Partial success: none.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js:47-56, 73-80, 388-395, 638-685`\n- `.pi/extensions/prompt-advisor.ts:1`\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts:292-296, 415-427`\n- `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/index.mjs` (no renderer match)\n\n## Assessment\n\n- New information ratio: 0.58\n- Questions addressed: per-runtime brief cost and health reachability\n- Questions answered: the per-runtime half of KQ2\n\n## Reflection\n\n- What worked and why: following the renderer once answered the byte question for all six runtimes, because every adapter delegates. The same read showed the opposite for health: the fields are per-adapter, so coverage cannot be assumed from the brief.\n- What did not work and why: grepping the DSH plugin for a renderer found nothing, which first looked like a gap. It is the expected shape — DSH consumes the shared CLI path — and the absence is the evidence.\n- What I would do differently: check the adapter's returned metadata before proposing a default, because the pass-through argument only holds where the timing already exists.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\n\nNone: the lane's three questions are answered and the remaining work is synthesis.\n","path":"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/ux-cost-3/iterations/iteration-005.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/ux-cost-3/iterations/iteration-005.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/ux-cost-3/iterations/iteration-005.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\":\"# Iteration 5: The brief is one renderer, but health is one runtime\\n\\n## Focus\\n\\nThe per-runtime half of the cost question: does the brief cost the same bytes on the plugin-shaped runtimes, and can OpenCode, Pi or DSH produce a health record at all? This bounds change 1's real coverage.\\n\\n## Focus Selection Check\\n\\nStrategy next focus named the per-runtime half after the implementation map. No exhausted approach applied. The pass reads three adapters and one constant table; it does not re-measure the Claude brief.\\n\\n## Actions Taken\\n\\n1. Read the OpenCode plugin's render path, its size caps and its directive-dedup mirror.\\n2. Read the Pi entry point to see whether it renders the brief or delegates.\\n3. Checked the plugin's returned metadata for the fields a health record would need, and for any timer.\\n4. Grepped the DSH plugin for a renderer or an `Advisor:` emitter.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n1. **Every runtime renders the same string from the same function.** The OpenCode plugin resolves the canonical renderer (`loadShadowRenderer`) and calls `renderAdvisorBrief` with the same options the hook passes, including the token cap and the threshold config; Pi re-exports the shared extension from the same skill; so for the same recommendation and freshness the emitted brief is byte-identical across runtimes. The per-runtime byte difference is zero, not a small number. [SOURCE: .opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js:638-668] [SOURCE: .pi/extensions/prompt-advisor.ts:1]\\n\\n2. **No runtime truncates a live brief.** The plugin's caps are 80 requested tokens (120 when ambiguous) and 2,048 brief characters, against a measured 296-byte median and an 80-token cap in the renderer, so a clamping path cannot fire on today's briefs. [SOURCE: .opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js:47-56] [SOURCE: .opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js:388-395]\\n\\n3. **The steady-state cost is runtime-wide, not Claude-only.** The plugin carries its own copy of the directive-lifecycle rule: full brief on the first message of a session and after a lifecycle boundary, the dynamic `Advisor:` route line only on a same-content repeat. So OpenCode also pays 218 bytes once and the 78-byte head afterwards. [SOURCE: .opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js:73-80]\\n\\n4. **The plugin already has most of what health needs, and not the two fields that matter.** Its brief envelope carries `freshness`, `cacheHit`, `skillLabel` and `recommendationCount`, but no `durationMs`, and it has no writer at all. A health record there needs a timer around the CLI spawn plus a writer call — a new subsystem rather than a pass-through. [SOURCE: .opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js:668-685]\\n\\n5. **The Claude hook is the only adapter whose result already carries the timing.** `emitDiagnostic` receives `result.metrics.durationMs` and `result.metrics.cacheHit` directly, which is why change 1 is two lines there and a new subsystem on the plugin runtimes. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts:415-427]\\n\\n6. **Change 1's coverage is four of six runtimes, and the limitation belongs in its own description.** Claude, Codex, Cursor and Devin share the hook adapter that emits diagnostics; OpenCode, Pi and DSH do not. An OpenCode-only user would still see no hook health after change 1, so the change removes the silent-empty case for the hook-shaped majority and not for everyone. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts:292-296]\\n\\n## Questions Answered\\n\\n- The per-runtime half of the cost question: zero byte difference for the same brief, and health is hook-adapter-only.\\n\\n## Questions Remaining\\n\\n- None in this lane. The cold-embedder sample stays a recorded gap.\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- **A per-runtime head to save bytes on hosts that resolve commands themselves.** Rejected again on the measurement: every host already gets the same 78-byte head, and segment one's rank 7 (a document path in the head) is the opposite direction, an addition rather than a cut.\\n- **Extending change 1 to the plugin runtimes in the same change.** Rejected as scope: it needs a timer, a writer and a new test surface on three adapters, and it would turn a two-line default flip into a multi-adapter build.\\n\\n## Dead Ends\\n\\n- Looking for a DSH renderer or an `Advisor:` emitter in `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/index.mjs` printed nothing, so DSH's brief arrives through the shared CLI path rather than a local renderer. That is consistent with the plugin finding and adds no separate cost.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- Ambiguous input: none.\\n- Contradictory evidence: none. Phase 013's claim that an OpenCode brief now names the command and policy is consistent with finding 1; this iteration adds that it also costs the same bytes.\\n- Missing dependencies: none.\\n- Partial success: none.\\n\\n## Sources Consulted\\n\\n- `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js:47-56, 73-80, 388-395, 638-685`\\n- `.pi/extensions/prompt-advisor.ts:1`\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts:292-296, 415-427`\\n- `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/index.mjs` (no renderer match)\\n\\n## Assessment\\n\\n- New information ratio: 0.58\\n- Questions addressed: per-runtime brief cost and health reachability\\n- Questions answered: the per-runtime half of KQ2\\n\\n## Reflection\\n\\n- What worked and why: following the renderer once answered the byte question for all six runtimes, because every adapter delegates. The same read showed the opposite for health: the fields are per-adapter, so coverage cannot be assumed from the brief.\\n- What did not work and why: grepping the DSH plugin for a renderer found nothing, which first looked like a gap. It is the expected shape — DSH consumes the shared CLI path — and the absence is the evidence.\\n- What I would do differently: check the adapter's returned metadata before proposing a default, because the pass-through argument only holds where the timing already exists.\\n\\n## Recommended Next Focus\\n\\nNone: the lane's three questions are answered and the remaining work is synthesis.\\n\",\"path\":\"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/ux-cost-3/iterations/iteration-005.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794551542044,"end":1794551561048}}}}
